  {
    "id": "form31_page-44",
    "text": "A 43-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 2-month history of intermittent abdominal pain. The pain occurs after fatty meals and is localized to the right upper quadrant. Ultrasonography of the abdomen shows cholelithiasis. The patient elects to begin a trial of ursodiol therapy before considering operative treatment. Which of the following is the primary mechanism of action of this medication?",
    "correct_answer": 1,
    "explanation": "The classic presentation of cholelithiasis is intermittent right upper quadrant pain and nausea that is worse with eating in a middle-aged female. Cholesterol gallstones are the most common type of gallstones, and risk factors for their development include obesity, advanced age, estrogen therapy, rapid weight loss, and Crohn disease. Inhibition of biliary cholesterol secretion is achieved by the use of ursodeoxycholic acid (ursodiol), a medication used to treat biliary colic, primary biliary cirrhosis, and intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy. In patients who have atypical symptoms that are not definitively attributable to gallstones, improvement with ursodiol suggests that cholecystectomy may be beneficial, but in patients with classic biliary colic, as in this patient, ursodiol is considered a second line to surgery. It works by reducing cholesterol secretion from the liver by 40% to 60%, and thereby reducing the cholesterol content of the bile. It also reduces the amount of cholesterol absorbed in the gut. As most gallstones are made of cholesterol, with a minority formed from heme because of chronic hemolysis, gradually reducing the amount of cholesterol in the bile prevents formation of new cholesterol gallstones.",
    "subject": "Biochemistry",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": 1,
        "text": "Decreased cholesterol secretion into bile",
        "letter": "A"
      },
      {
        "id": 2,
        "text": "Enhanced cholesterol absorption",
        "letter": "B"
      },
      {
        "id": 3,
        "text": "Enhanced hepatic cholesterol synthesis",
        "letter": "C"
      },
      {
        "id": 4,
        "text": "Inhibition of bile acid synthesis",
        "letter": "D"
      },
      {
        "id": 5,
        "text": "Inhibition of 3-HMG-CoA",
        "letter": "E"
      }
    ]
  },
